I keep F4, I can aim for a Pung of F4 (North Wind), which would give me both Seat Wind and Prevalent Wind. Since I already have two, getting another would be good. Also, having a Pung of F4 would add points. \\n\\nAlternatively, if I discard F4, I might be able to form other patterns. But given that F4 is my seat wind and the prevailing wind, it's a valuable tile. \\n\\nLooking at the melds, I have a Chi of T1-T2-T3. That's a Chow in Bamboo. Maybe I can form more Chows in another suit. For example, if I can get W1-W2-W3 as a Chow. But I have W1, W2, and one W3. If I discard a W3, I can't form that. So keeping W3 is important for that possibility. \\n\\nSo, if I discard F4, I lose the chance to form a Pung of F4. If I discard W3, I might lose the chance to form a Chow or Pung in W. \\n\\nLooking at the valid actions, the options include Play F4, B7, F2, W3, etc. \\n\\nWhich of these is the safest to discard? \\n\\nB7 and B9 are single tiles. Discarding them might be safe. But if I discard B7 or B9, maybe someone else can use them. However, since there are multiple remaining, it's possible. \\n\\nBut considering the value of F4, I should keep it. So maybe discard B7 or B9. Let me check the unshown tiles for B7 and B9. \\n\\nUnshown B7: 4 remaining. B9: 4 remaining. So discarding either is safe. \\n\\nBut I also have B4. B4 has 3 remaining. If I discard B4, maybe someone can use it. But B4 is part of a possible Chow with B3 and B5. Since I don't have those, it's not helpful. \\n\\nSo between the options, discarding B7 or B9 seems safe. Let's see if there's any other consideration. \\n\\nLooking at my hand, I have F2 and F4. F2 is South Wind. Since the prevailing wind is North, having a Pung of F4 is better. So maybe keeping F2 isn't useful. But discarding F2 would leave me with one less tile. However, if someone else has a Pung of South Wind, they might need it, but I don't know. \\n\\nAlternatively, if I discard W1, W2, or W3, which are part of possible sequences. \\n