ince F4 is more valuable. \\n\\nBut the valid actions include Play F2. \\n\\nSo the options are: \\n\\n- Play F4: but that's part of a potential Pung. \\n\\n- Play B7: single tile. \\n\\n- Play B9: single tile. \\n\\n- Play W3: part of possible Chow or Pung. \\n\\nI think the best choice is to discard B7 or B9. Let's pick one. Maybe B7. \\n\\nBut why not consider other factors? Let's check for possible patterns. \\n\\nIf I discard B7, my hand becomes: W1, W2, W3(2), W4, B4, B9, F2, F4(2). \\n\\nIf I discard B9, my hand becomes: W1, W2, W3(2), W4, B4, B7, F2, F4(2). \\n\\nEither way, the key is to keep the W3s for possible Pung or Chow. \\n\\nSo between B7 and B9, which is better to discard? \\n\\nLooking at the unshown tiles, B7 has 4 remaining, B9 has 4. So no difference in safety. \\n\\nMaybe discard B7. \\n\\nAlternatively, maybe there's a better reason to discard another tile. \\n\\nWait, I also have B4. B4 has 3 remaining. Discarding B4 might be an option. But B4 is part of a possible Chow with B3 and B5. Since I don't have those, it's not helpful. \\n\\nSo the best options are B7 or B9. \\n\\nI think I'll go with discarding B7. So the action is Play B7.\\n\", \"output\": \"Evaluation: Discarding B7 is a strategic move as it is a single tile not contributing to any immediate high-value patterns, while preserving potential for forming Pungs or Chows in W3 and F4.\\nReason: Keeping W3 supports potential for a Pung or Chow in Characters, and retaining F4 (North Wind, matching both seat and prevalent wind) offers scoring opportunities. Discarding B7, a non-contributory tile, minimizes risk without sacrificing future hand development.\\nAnswer: Play B7\"}"}
